<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gubbio</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iguvium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gubbio, the ancient Iguvium, is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the slopes of Monte Ingino. It was an important Umbrian community and then a Roman municipium, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iguvine-tablets">Iguvine Tablets</a> found there in 1444 preserve its ancient religious rituals. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a free commune before passing to the dukes of Urbino, and its medieval palaces and Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gubbio.jpg" alt="Gubbio" width="320" height="118" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Labicanense · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gubbio_panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
